[THOUGHT]  
The clinical presentation is highly characteristic of autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD):  
- Age range (45–60 years) aligns with typical age of symptom onset (flank pain, hypertension, renal enlargement);  
- 5-month history of intermittent flank pain reflects cyst expansion or hemorrhage;  
- Bilateral nontender upper abdominal masses correspond to massively enlarged, cystic kidneys;  
- Renal ultrasound shows *bilaterally enlarged kidneys with multiple parenchymal anechoic masses* — the imaging hallmark of ADPKD;  
- Hypertension (150/88 mmHg) is present in >70% of ADPKD patients, often preceding significant eGFR decline;  
- Elevated serum creatinine (1.4 mg/dL) indicates early CKD (G2), consistent with progressive cyst-mediated nephron loss;  
- Recurrent UTIs (5 episodes in 2 years) are a known complication due to cyst infection or urinary stasis;  
- Absence of red-flag symptoms (no fever, weight loss, hematuria, or systemic illness) argues against malignancy, vasculitis, or acute interstitial nephritis;  
- No personal/family history of kidney disease is *not* inconsistent with ADPKD — up to 25% of cases arise from *de novo* PKD1 mutations, and family history may be unknown or underreported.  

All other differential diagnoses (e.g., acquired cystic kidney disease, von Hippel–Lindau, tuberous sclerosis complex, medullary sponge kidney) are excluded by:  
- Bilateral *parenchymal* (not just medullary) anechoic cysts;  
- Kidney *enlargement* (not normal or small kidneys);  
- Absence of extrarenal manifestations (e.g., retinal angiomas, facial angiofibromas, liver cysts not mentioned but not required for diagnosis);  
- Early-onset hypertension and recurrent UTIs — more typical of ADPKD than other cystic diseases.  

Ultrasound findings meet established diagnostic criteria for ADPKD in at-risk individuals: ≥3 unilateral or bilateral cysts by age 30–59 years (Pei et al. 2009 criteria), especially with supportive clinical features (hypertension, flank pain, family history — even if unconfirmed). Given the classic triad of imaging, hypertension, and symptomatic renal enlargement, ADPKD is definitive.

[DIAGNOSIS]  
Autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease (ADPKD)

